// Outer voices -- soprano and bass -- from J.S. Bach's 371 four-part chorales.
// Powers the rhythm trainer's borrowed rhythms, and melody mode for one or both hands.
//
// Source encodings: the **kern edition at github.com/craigsapp/bach-370-chorales.
// The music is public domain (Bach, d. 1750). Only notated pitches and durations are
// taken, read from the **kern spines located by name so a lyric spine cannot shift the
// columns; no lyrics or other text are read. Pitches keep the source's own spelling so
// accidentals render correctly rather than being guessed back from a pitch number.
// A measure is kept only if BOTH voices are playable here: 4/4, no tuplets, and no dot
// or tie the renderer can't spell -- a single dot on a quarter, eighth or half note is
// fine (matches DURATION_BEATS in rhythm.js), a double dot or a dot on anything else
// isn't. Each voice has to sum to exactly one bar. Ties are kept too, but only where the
// note they carry into actually survives in this corpus -- a tie into a dropped measure,
// or across a gap between two runs, is stripped rather than left dangling. Consecutive
// keepers stay grouped so an excerpt is a real contiguous passage (minimum two bars),
// not unrelated bars stitched together.
//
// Every excerpt also carries 'vocab' -- the exact set of duration codes it uses, in
// rhythm.js's own scheme ('q', '8d', ...) -- and 'ties', whether it contains one at all.
// Neither is consulted by anything yet; they exist so a future difficulty toggle ("no
// dotted quarters", say) can filter which excerpts are eligible by checking this field
// directly rather than re-parsing every note token in the corpus to find out.
//
// Generated by a rebuild after tie and dot support landed in the renderer/scorer -- see
// rhythm.js's add_ties()/add_cross_measure_ties() and fill_beat()'s dotted-split branch
// for the generated-rhythm side of the same features, and bach_excerpt()'s post-slice
// tie guard for why a tie can still vanish here at practice time even though the corpus
// itself keeps it: a random excerpt window can still land a kept tie on a rendered
// system break, which is stripped there, not here.
//
// Generated, do not hand-edit.
//   BACH_NOTES     distinct 'duration:pitch' tokens ('8r' = eighth rest, 'qd' = dotted
//                  quarter, trailing '~' = tied to the next note)
//   BACH_MEASURES  distinct one-bar measures, as arrays of BACH_NOTES indices
//   BACH_PIECES    [BWV number, chorale title, key signature]
//   BACH_EXCERPTS  {p: piece index, s: soprano measures, b: bass measures,
//                  vocab: duration codes used, ties: whether it has one at all}

